            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="false" heatedGarage="true" portableAirConditioning="false" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="0" energyStarUnits="0" evChargingStation="false" outdoorGasAppliances="false" commonSpace="false" elevator="false" fitnessRoom="false" snowmeltSystem="false" unheatedGarage="false" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="1" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>The infiltration test has shown air leakage from the following house components:

- Receptacles

- Some windows

- All doors

- Skylight

- Exposed floor

- Combustion air supply and furnace chimney

During the infiltration test walkthrough, we notice air leakage on the items listed above. Airtightness is very important to achieve energy savings.

</airSeal>
                <ceilings>At the time of the audit, the attic insulation value was estimated to be R-28 with 8?????????????????? of fiberglass insulation batt. We estimated the insulation level according to the year of construction. we recommend adding insulation to meet the city of Whitehorse standard of R-60. We recommend the same thing for the cathedral ceiling</ceilings>
                <exposedFloors>The floor above the garage is considered as an exposed floor and could represent a big portion of your heat loss. A solution to reduce the energy loss would be to increase the insulation of the floor above garage and sealed it to improve the airtightness of the floor. </exposedFloors>
                <windows>We recommend the replacement of the windows by triple pane and Energy Star certified units with Lowe coating and Argon filled.</windows>
                <doors>We recommend the replacement of the wood doors with new energy star certified products</doors>
                <AdditionalComments>The depressurization test performed as shown a high risk of combustion gas spillage. Therefore, we recommend verifying your carbon monoxide detectors regularly.</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
